-- title:
--   ℤ₍ₚ₎[1/j] is integrally closed with fraction field ℚ(j)
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a natural number carrying the assumption that it is prime. Work inside the field $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$ of formal Laurent series in $q$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, and let $jq$ be the Laurent series $q^{-1}$ times the image under $\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Q}$ of the integral power series $jNum$, i.e. the $q$-expansion $q^{-1}+744+\dots$ of the modular invariant. Write $R$ for the subring [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt p`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) of $\mathbb{Q}$ consisting of those rationals whose denominator is coprime to $p$ (so $R=\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$), let $A=\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\,R\,\{jq^{-1}\}$ be the $R$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the inverse of $jq$, and let $\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle$ be the intermediate field of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by $jq$. The assertion is the conjunction of two statements. First, every $x$ in $\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle$ that is integral over $A$ already lies in $A$; that is, $A$ is integrally closed in $\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle$. Second, for every $x$ in $\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle$ there exist $b,s\in A$ with $s\neq 0$ and $s\,x=b$; that is, every element of $\mathbb{Q}\langle jq\rangle$ is a fraction of elements of $A$.
--
--   This is the level-one case of the pole chart of the two-chart integral model of $X_0(M)$ over $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$: for $M=1$ the curve is the $j$-line, and on the chart where $1/j$ is regular the coordinate ring is $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}[1/j]$, here realised concretely via $q$-expansions. It feeds the chart computations for the Igusa scheme, being used by [`ModularCurve.IgusaScheme.exists_mul_eq_natCast_mul_add_jInvChartInf_mul_of_coeff_zero_mem`](thm.html#ModularCurve.IgusaScheme.exists_mul_eq_natCast_mul_add_jInvChartInf_mul_of_coeff_zero_mem); its proof cites the transcendence of $jq$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ together with the facts that $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ is a discrete valuation ring with fraction field $\mathbb{Q}$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_mem_adjoin_jq_inv_of_isIntegral_and_exists_mul_eq.lean

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_X0
import Definitions.Def_GaloisRep_Flat

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
